xigmatek-connect-lcd (0.7) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * The offline checks move out of one shell file and into ordinary test files.
    `tools/selftest.sh` - 13,869 lines in 22 sections, grown over six cycles -
    is deleted, and what it checked is 435 checks across 46 files in `tests/`
    as that work landed, run by the standard library: `python3 -m unittest
    discover -s tests`.  What the tree holds today, and the runs that printed
    it, are in the cycle's own bullet at the end of this entry.  Every timing
    is the runner's own and says which box it ran on.
    The author's bench, one sitting, the same tree both ways: 124.240 s for
    the 278 checks as they stood at the deletion, 353.945 s for the 435 they
    are now.  CI's runner, the `tests/` step alone: 323 s for the 414 at
    0afc2cc, and for the 435 four runs came out 381 s, 419 s, 440 s and
    447 s - 43171703533, 43171666546, 43169813952, 43177293979 - the spread
    being the runner's rather than the checks', printed because one sample of
    it would read like a measurement.  Against that, the 497 s the same runner
    spent on the suite's single step for `v0.6`.  The step's own neighbours,
    over those runs: 28-36 s to import what the checks touch, 0-1 s for the
    manifest pass, 0 s for shellcheck, 40-83 s to build the two .deb files,
    17-20 s to install them and 1-2 s for the packaged smoke test; publish
    stayed skipped on every one of them, as it must until a tag exists.
    Nothing that examined code was dropped in the end - the wire, the
    encoder's loss budget, the copied library's painting, the export parity,
    the substitutions a layout names, `alpha`'s special rows, the window, the
    editor's model and its doors, the control socket, the panel worker's
    gates, the background pairing.  That claim did not hold at the first cut,
    and the way to find that out was to read the deleted file rather than the
    changelog: twelve sections had gone unported.  They are back as
    `test_tooltips.py`, `test_apply_layout.py`, `test_snapping.py`,
    `test_row_resize.py`, `test_peek_readings.py`, `test_row_acts.py`,
    `test_row_notes.py`, `test_fixture_shape.py`, `test_field_census.py`,
    `test_bars_rows.py`, `test_recorded_series.py` and
    `test_names_and_renames.py`, plus three checks in files that already
    existed - 136 in all, each shown non-vacuous by breaking the shipped
    code it examines and counting the failures.  What was deliberately left
    behind is everything that existed to re-quote a figure or a sentence from
    a document, and the six `docs/videos` files whose only gate measured
    their own frame count, length and size.

  * Four sections that were still in the deleted suite, ported because they
    examine code rather than documents.  `test_field_census.py` holds the
    census behind `--print-theme-fields`: 152 widgets in the 27 copied
    layouts answering to 33 `property` keys, the four names `layout.py`
    reads with no shipped file to show for them, the eleven the files carry
    that nothing reads (each with a reason in `themes.UNREAD_WHY`), and the
    rule that the extension keys the painter was found to read are exactly
    the keys with an entry in `themes.XIGMA_KEYS`.  `test_bars_rows.py` asks
    `bars` from both sides - the strings its painter writes, taken out of
    the drawing by spying on `ImageDraw.text` for one frame, against the
    strings `layout.text_for` prints from the same source names - and counts
    the ten reserved row slots in the pixels, so a box reporting no
    tachometer gets the same pitch as one reporting six.
    `test_recorded_series.py` keeps the ledger beside the published clips:
    the twelve-sample series has to record the zone its on-glass clock was
    read at, because one instant of epoch milliseconds prints `00:25:16` on
    this bench and `22:25:16` on a UTC runner, and every `bar` row that
    recording could have had is answered at every one of its samples.
    `test_names_and_renames.py` holds the name rules and the rename act 0.6
    shipped with: the limit usable to its edge and refused one past it in
    words that name it, `why_taken` answering for a name, its fold, its
    swapcase and its id, a fresh id thirteen digits and above every answered
    one, and a rename that moves the id and nothing else - the rest of the
    file byte-identical, the pixels unmoved, the vendor's layout answering
    again at its own path, the `.bak` travelling with the copy while a
    crash's `.tmp` stays where it was left.

  * A comment in the shipped source names the check that holds the claim it
    makes, or says plainly that nothing does.  Sixty mentions of the deleted
    suite are gone from `xigmalcd/`, `xigmaui/`, `tools/` and `tests/`, and
    following them out turned up claims pointing at gates that had never
    existed.  The `.door` marker on each `themes.SPECIALS` drawing: two
    checks in `test_specials.py` now read every marker against the
    `layout.draw_special_*` call inside that same body, which is the only
    way a listing printed by `--list-specials` can stop being a lie.  The
    editor's row pickers vanishing with a form that was built and never
    installed: `test_window.py` walks the editor's own box and asks for the
    three rows by name, because "no exception" is not how you notice a
    missing control.  And the name-length limit, where the comment said the
    window caps its box at the writer's limit while `xigmaui/app.py` says
    the opposite on purpose - a box that stops at the limit drops characters
    and then presses the button with a name nobody typed - so the refusal is
    now gated at the edge and one past it, in words that quote the constant.

  * Re-pointing the last three pages at their owners turned up five claims no
    check held.  They are gated rather than downgraded.  `test_pane_chrome.py`:
    the selection chrome never reaches the frame - the pane grabbed with a mark
    up and without one has to differ, which is what proves the chrome is painted
    at all, while the pixmap the label is handed and the image the model painted
    are the same either way, and a repaint brings the mark back for the row that
    is still selected; a value menu shows the value the row holds, walked
    through the pane's own widget picker over the fields whose stored answer is
    not the vocabulary's first, because the old bug hid in a case where it was;
    the tray's theme list is the window's list, which skips out loud under
    `QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen` instead of passing quietly; and the upload chord
    stays the window's while a field holds focus and moves no text.
    `test_bars_rows.py` gained the generated file's own structure, derived from
    `BARS_ROWS` and the `BARS_*` constants rather than remembered: the widget
    count and the kind tally that follows from them, the label, number and meter
    answering for one place on the glass with the meter `BARS_BAR_DY` under its
    row, one convention pair on every meter, and `showIf` on exactly the rows
    whose number prints one field while its meter divides two.  Its two memory
    rules are measured at the five readings that found them - a total with no
    used figure keeps the row's label and draws no number, where it once raised
    `KeyError` inside the frame loop, and a GPU reporting exactly 0 used keeps
    its row and reads `0 GB`, where its test used to require a truthy numerator
    - and the key's worth is measured by taking it away at an integrated GPU's
    readings and requiring the picture to change.  The review door's two lists
    are held to each other, which is the shape a runner with no GPU hits: a row
    silenced by its dependency leaves its source in `cannot:` with no skip
    behind it, and a box that answers everything skips that relation out loud.
    `test_export_parity.py` put the deleted suite's worst near-miss back under a
    check: a reading is an input, not a scratch pad.  Neither `themes.parity`,
    `render.draw` nor `themes.values` writes to the body it is handed, over all
    five themes and all five shared readings, and a check that wants a hole asks
    for one on a copy - the mistake a gate once made in the shared fixture, and
    paid for with every later draw in the process.
    And the naming rule holds itself: `test_docs_links.py` re-reads every
    `tests/<file>` pointer in the published prose - 78 mentions over 43 files -
    and in the shipped comments, against the tracked file list, requires a named
    `test_*.py` to hold at least one `def test_`, and asserts how many pointers
    it read, so a scan that found nothing is a failure rather than a pass.  Each
    of these was shown non-vacuous by breaking the shipped code it examines: an
    exported meter moved 3 px off the drawing's -> 10 subtests; `pct_pair` made
    to raise again for a missing numerator, and made to treat a 0 reading as no
    reading -> 1 each; an export with `showIf` already stripped, so stripping
    has nothing left to show -> 1; `themes.parity` made to pop a key out of the
    caller's dict -> 25; `themes.values` made to tidy its argument -> 5; a
    repaint that stops re-applying the selection's chrome -> 1; a menu landed on
    index 0 -> 1; the chord moved back to a bare Ctrl+U -> 1; and a page pointed
    at a test file that is not in the tree, or at a tracked module holding no
    check -> 1 each.  Two claims the pages made that no check ever held are
    stated as gaps where they stand, with the door to re-measure each named: the
    six published `docs/videos` files, whose staleness nothing scores any more
    (`ffprobe`, `du -b`), and the display loop's rename race
    (`xigmalcd --theme <copy>`).

  * `tests/fixtures.py` holds the isolation and the pinning once, which the
    suite rebuilt inside each of its 22 sections: the repo root taken from this
    file, `QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen` before anything imports Qt, a temporary
    `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` for the process and again per test, `XIGMA_SOCKET` pointed
    at a path that does not exist so the window asks a socket before it would
    ask a port, one pinned instant and four pinned bodies of readings, a
    recording stub panel with deliberately no `send`, and the pixel helpers so a
    "same picture" claim always answers with pixels and worst channel.  The
    guard the old suite closed with - the sha256 of the real
    `~/.config/xigmaui/config.json` unchanged - is `tests/test_zz_isolation.py`,
    and its failure message names the two possible causes instead of just the
    one.

  * `tools/verify_manifest.py` replaces the suite's largest non-GUI section, the
    2,910 lines titled "the vendor theme library: every copied byte against its
    own manifest".  It re-hashes all 125 `files[]` entries - 27 backgrounds, 27
    foregrounds, 27 layouts, 44 clips, 128,106,463 B - cross-checks `counts`
    against `files` and against the tree, reports anything sitting in a scanned
    directory that the manifest does not name, matches each layout's declared
    background against the copied clip's hash, and asserts that the 27 encrypted
    `.thm` containers XIGMATEK owns are recorded and absent: 66,300,082 B of
    bytes this repository documents and does not ship.  0.12 s over the real
    tree, and shown non-vacuous against a fake root with one deleted file, one
    changed file, one extra file and one present container.

  * A page stops carrying a figure that needs a gate per machine.  Where the
    suite checked that a number in `docs/` equalled what this box measured, the
    figure comes out of the page and the page names the command that prints it -
    `xigmalcd --list-themes`, `tools/hotpath.py`, `tools/theme_check.py`,
    `xigmalcd --version`.  That is what makes the document cuts in this release
    safe rather than quietly stale, and it is also why the gates that counted
    sections and re-read changelog sentences are gone rather than rewritten.

  * Four fixes in the shipped code, each one surfaced by running a check
    rather than by reading for style, and all four the same habit: reading a
    file and leaving the handle to the garbage collector.
    `xigmalcd/protocol.py`: `usb_label` read three sysfs attributes that
    way, on every port scan and every session renewal - measured over one
    `find_ports()` pass, 3 unclosed-file warnings before and 0 after, with
    the answer unchanged (`/dev/ttyACM0  Xigmatek USB Device [1d6b:0109]`).
    `xigmalcd/main.py`: the background upload read the whole file the same
    way, in the loop that renews its session every 60 s.
    `xigmalcd/themes.py`: `shape_of` and `copy_for_edit` read a layout file
    the same way, on every editor open and every copy for editing - 27
    unclosed handles over one pass of the copied library, 0 after.  And
    `xigmalcd/main.py` defined `_run_action` **twice**, byte-identical,
    since the baseline commit: one definition left, 33 lines of dead code
    gone.

  * CI gains `tests/` and `tools/verify_manifest.py` as steps, and a
    `shellcheck` step over the shell that ships: measured here,
    `install/install.sh` has no findings.  The job's `timeout-minutes` and apt
    retries came from the 0.6 cycle; what changed since the tag is that a job
    now fails fast rather than sitting six hours on a runner that cannot resolve
    `*.ubuntu.com`.

  * Version parity is one assertion over three places a reader meets the number
    - `xigmalcd.__version__`, `pyproject.toml`, and the top `debian/changelog`
    stanza - rather than a line inside the deleted suite.  The deb job still
    compares the changelog with the tag, which only exists during a build.

  * Three fixes to the layout editor's drag, and a command to measure them.
    Dragging a row was doing a whole frame's work per mouse-move: it re-read
    this machine's sensors on every event, it repainted the clip behind the
    pane, and it re-ran the window's sizing pass.  The complaint came from the
    desktop, so the cost has a command now: `tools/dragcost.py` drags one row
    at a named rate and prints milliseconds per move, driven through the
    window's own pane-readings knob rather than by setting state behind it, and
    it answers in each of the three modes - `fixture` and `sensors` measure
    (this bench, 2026-11-12: mean 10.8 and 11.4 ms per move over 20 moves),
    `file` refuses the press and exits 2, because a held metrics file is a look
    rather than a reading and twenty moves of nothing would print as though it
    had been measured.

  * `bars` rows overlapped on the glass, not only in the pane: the meter moved
    and the track came down.  That change took the only visibly-overlapping pair
    of rows out of the shipped corpus, and with it the loud half of the reorder
    census in `tests/test_row_acts.py`.  It is named in the page rather than
    papered over, and then rebuilt on purpose: `AVisibleReorderIsBuiltOnPurpose`
    builds rows that overlap and holds them to the same standard the census
    does, so the law still has a loud case to fail on.

  * The README's three window pictures come from one command now, offscreen:
    `tools/screenshot.py`.  Two of them had been live captures of the author's
    desktop, which is why they went stale - one was made 2026-11-08, before the
    row acts, the peek and the geometry change - and a live capture of a status
    box paints this panel's serial into the pixels.  The pictures are generated
    instead, and `tests/test_no_serial.py` reads them back with OCR: the serial
    appears in no published text and no published pixel.

  * CI's red was two faults, and one of them was the suite's own.  A thumbnail
    check called the window's populate door a second time, and that door
    *creates* its interval-0 timer - so the window's first timer was left
    running while the check stopped only the newest one, and the orphan went on
    firing two subprocesses a tick through whichever test file ran next.  The
    file it starved was the one holding a 2 s deadline.  Fixed on both sides:
    `_populate_clips` makes that timer once for the life of the window, a check
    counts the timers by name and identity after a second populate (the pre-fix
    form put back fails 1 of the 5, as shipped 0), and the tooltip helper waits
    for the widget to become visible, repeats the request, and counts
    **event-loop spins** rather than seconds.  A cost gate asserts a count.

  * The pane-readings choice moved across the window to where the picture it
    chooses is: a label, a drop-down and a path field between the `playing` box
    and the preview pane, and it no longer folds away with the knobs that write
    settings - a choice about the picture is not one of those.  It stays shut
    rather than hidden while no layout is open, and its path field is live only
    in the `a metrics file` mode.  The folded-control census in
    `tests/test_window.py` went 12 -> 9 with it.

  * The frame loop and the editor's pane painted one layout file with two
    different value tables, and that is how a saved copy of `alpha` lost its
    temperature and fan figures on the glass while the pane went on showing
    them.  `themes.paint`, the loop's door, never called `fill_lines` - which is
    where the six composite keys a `special` row reads come from: `temp-cpu`,
    `temp-gpu`, `fan-cpu`, `fan-gpu`, `readings`, `status`.  No file spells
    those names as a `source` and `validate` has nothing to say about them, so
    every registered drawing that found its pair empty returned **no box at
    all**, quietly.  Measured on this bench at one pinned body and instant, the
    old door paints `alpha` 11 167 px lighter (`temp-values` 7 251 px and
    `fan-figures` 2 234 px down to nothing), `clock` 4 687 px and `dual` 1 851
    px; `temp-labels` survived because its words are constants rather than
    readings, which is what made the loss look like one bad row instead of four.
    One private door now, `themes._overlay`, called by both - the canvas stays
    an explicit argument, because that is the one place the doors genuinely
    differ.  And a door that stops filling can no longer be quiet: the notes
    carry `special:<name>` with the reason, and `--check-theme` and
    `apply to the panel` ask the loop's door from now on, so their counts are
    the file's and not the buffer under the cursor's.  `tests/test_specials.py`
    10 -> 15, `tests/test_apply_layout.py` 7 -> 8, parity held over all five
    generated layouts *saved as files*, and the registry's keys checked against
    `SPECIAL_LINES` with `ast` in both directions.  Shown non-vacuous: shared
    door stops filling -> 4 failures; the notes go quiet -> 1; a drawing reads a
    key nothing fills -> 48; `SPECIAL_LINES` loses a figure key -> 47; either
    door ignores the size it declares -> 1 each; `apply` counting the buffer ->
    1.  Back as shipped, 0.

  * The window gained the two doors a report said it lacked: "I discovered a bug
    where i have no way of opening & loading a previously saved layout in
    xigmaui".  Inside that sentence were two faults.  The layout menu was a
    snapshot, built when the window was and rebuilt only after a rename, a
    save-as or a revert *in that window*, so a copy that appeared by any other
    door - the CLI, a second window, a file dropped into
    `~/.config/xigmalcd/themes` - stayed invisible until a restart: `reload the
    list` re-runs `themes.scan()`, and the sentence it prints counts out of
    `themes.scan()` and `themes.user_copies()` at that moment rather than from a
    number written into the source.  And a layout file outside the three roots
    `themes.resolve` reads - one copied off another machine, the
    `<name>.json.bak` a save leaves beside a copy, the stdout of
    `--export-theme` pointed at a file - could not be opened at all: `open a
    file…`, through `edit.Editor.open_path`, which writes nothing, and sends
    to the one writable directory whatever it opened, and refuses in words that
    quote the path - nothing there, a directory, not JSON, JSON that parses but
    carries no `osd`/`widget`, and a 4:3 page in `themes.RATIO_WHY`'s own
    sentence.  What the new door cannot do, it says: applying a layout sends a
    *name*, and a display resolves that name in the roots *it* can see, so a
    layout that only a path answers is refused before anything goes and the line
    under the menu names which of three cases applies - nothing answers the
    name, your own copy does, or somebody else's file answers the same stem.
    `Editor.opened` keeps the file a buffer was read from apart from where a
    save would go, and one wording, `edit.PATH_ORIGIN`, is quoted by the state
    line, the refusal and the checks.  Thirteen checks in
    `tests/test_open_layouts.py`; ten ways to break the code on purpose, and
    what each cost, are written in docs/theme-editor.md.

  * The glass driver has the phase the door fix owed: `APPLY a layout file` in
    `tools/display_glass.py` writes a copy with `themes.save`, names it over the
    socket, and asks the loop's own painter what it found - one line per
    `special` row with its box - with a row that comes back box-less recorded as
    a **failed phase**.  Counters moving was never evidence here: the rows
    were not drawn wrong, they were not drawn.  The phase is gated
    statically by `tests/test_glass_ledger.py` (5 -> 6); the run itself still
    needs a panel, and docs/limits.md's stage-6 ledger says so.

  * A page stops carrying figures no command can reprint.  docs/gui.md quoted
    twelve pixel figures from the window's sizing pass, labelled as two boxes'
    readings from 2026-11-11; one of its three column floors had already moved
    and nothing could re-derive any of them.  `tests/test_window_sizing.py`
    prints its own census now - the floors, the split, how many columns of rows
    the field grid laid itself out into and what that grid asked for, at three
    window widths, and `app.py`'s lower bounds - and the page names the command
    instead of the numbers.  It also gained the check that the two new buttons
    under the layout menu cost that column no width: the buttons together are
    narrower than the widest entry the menu has to stay readable for, and hiding
    them moves the floor by 0 px.  Both boxes printed their own pair in the
    same pass: 328 / 376 / 373 px of column floors at a 1280 px window here,
    331 / 394 / 390 there.

  * The .deb files this cycle builds were installed and read back.  Built on
    this bench from the tree above (`dh_clean`, then `dpkg-buildpackage -us
    -uc -b`): code 5 441 716 B, themes 79 875 580 B, one mtime each, `lintian
    --fail-on error,warning` with no tags, and the two fixes present in the
    packaged bytes - `themes._overlay` called by both painters, and the
    window's `open a file…`.  Installed on the owner's desktop, the same
    `alpha_new.json` that through 0.6 reported two of its twelve widgets with
    no box at all now prints, from `/usr/bin/xigmalcd`: 12 boxes, 40 390 lit
    px, `skipped: nothing`, drawn through `themes.paint` - the door the frame
    loop uses for a layout file.  That is the installed loop's own painter
    reporting what it found on a box with a panel attached; the panel still
    has no `APPLY a layout file` transcript, and says so below.

  * The check that runs against an installed system had never run on a machine
    that had been used.  `debian/tests/smoke`'s last section added up what
    `themes.scan()` returned and compared that to the layouts the package's
    own manifest lists; on the owner's desktop, with one saved copy of their
    own in `~/.config/xigmalcd/themes`, it came out 24 + 4 against 27 and the
    install was called broken for a reason that belonged to the check.
    `themes.roots()` puts your directory in front of the installed library on
    purpose - that is what makes a copy you edited mean `--theme <name>` - so
    27 files and 28 names are both correct.  The accounting is per root now:
    every installed layout file is either answered from the package and
    selectable, refused with a printed reason, or **hidden behind a copy of
    yours at the same name**, and that last state is named out loud because it
    is the one a person is usually in without noticing.  The paint loop still
    walks everything selectable, copies included, since a copy is the file a
    loop actually draws; the pairing claim belongs to the package, so it walks
    the package's own records - a copy of `alpha` declares no background, and
    that is a fact about your file rather than a broken install.  Three states
    pinned: a bare root 23 selectable, 4 refused, 0 hidden; one copy at a new
    name 23, 4, 0 with 1 in front; one copy at a vendor's own name 22, 4, 1.
    And because that script ships inside the source tree while interrogating
    the **installed** code, it now names the mismatch before it measures
    anything: on a bench with 0.6 installed it answers that the `xigmalcd` it
    is about to interrogate has no such rule and this checkout and this
    install are not the same code, and stops with exit 1 instead of failing a
    count on somebody's healthy system.  That was the sentence missing when
    this cycle's report arrived, and the static check beside it holds the
    wording.

  * The same block then found a fault in the shipped code, and it was a reader
    missing a rule the writer has had since 0.6.  `themes.save` refuses to put
    down a layout with an empty `osd.widget` list, but `scan()` selected on
    the `ratio` field alone: a JSON that parses, declares 480x480 and carries
    no `osd` at all - somebody's metrics dump, a clip's sidecar, half a config
    file - was a name `resolve()` answered, and `layout.draw_theme` walks
    `obj["osd"]["widget"]`, so the frame naming it raised `KeyError: 'osd'`
    instead of drawing an empty canvas.  `scan()` marks such a record unusable
    with a `why` and a `why_code` now, `resolve()` refuses by that same mark,
    and `why_not()`, `--theme`, `--check-theme` and the window's `open a
    file…` all print one sentence, `themes.NEED_OSD_WHY`; `--list-themes`
    counts the reasons out - `23 pickable, 5 refused: 4 on canvas, 1 with
    nothing to draw` - and names the file it refused.  What was deliberately
    not done: `scan()` does not run `validate()`.  The loop re-resolves its
    theme name on every frame, and validating 152 widgets every frame is a
    cost this repository has never measured, so the reader asks the one
    predicate the painter actually needs and `--check-theme` stays the door
    that prints the detailed report.  That asymmetry is written as a limit in
    docs/limits.md rather than left undeclared.

  * Six checks for those two, one of which runs the other file.
    `tests/test_theme_files.py` 5 -> 6: the unusable file is excluded and
    listed, `resolve()` says None for its stem *and* for its id, `--theme` and
    `--check-theme` exit 2 quoting the rule, and `--list-themes` prints the
    file, the reason and the new tally.  `tests/test_smoke_accounting.py`, new
    with 5 checks, extracts the accounting block out of `debian/tests/smoke`
    and runs it against this checkout's copy of the same tree, in the three
    states above and with the junk file, pointing the block's CLI cross-check
    at this tree's entry point rather than at whatever `xigmalcd` the package
    manager left on PATH - and reads the script itself, so a per-root identity
    cannot quietly become a bare count again.  Shown to bite: put the
    pristine-home identity back and 2 of the 5 fail, plus the static one; take
    the new reader rule out and the theme-files check fails on all four doors
    at once.

  * An upgrade on a logged-in session, observed rather than reasoned about.
    The owner's desktop answered `systemctl --user restart xigmalcd` with
    `Warning: The unit file, source configuration file or drop-ins of
    xigmalcd.service changed on disk. Run 'systemctl --user daemon-reload' to
    reload units.`  Neither .deb has a `postinst`, so nothing in an install
    speaks to the session's user manager - that manager belongs to the
    desktop, not to dpkg - and the restart therefore ran the new
    `/usr/bin/xigmalcd` under the old unit's properties.  Here the two unit
    files agree on `ExecStart`, so nothing changed; the order that provably
    loads a changed unit is `stop`, `daemon-reload`, `start`, and
    `install/install.sh`, which writes the checkout's own copy of the unit,
    already does all three.  docs/packaging.md records it beside the 0.2
    observation that an upgrade can start the display on its own.

  * Three reports about the editor's save-and-apply workflow, and the one rule
    all three sit on: a display is only ever told a *name*, never a layout
    file (PROTOCOL.md §11).  "save as your copy immediately applies the
    textColor change … in alpha_new" was the design working: the loop an
    earlier apply had moved to that name re-resolves it every frame, so the
    save alone put new bytes in front of it.  "apply to the panel has no
    effect, if pressed before save as your copy" was true in effect, and the
    window had been reporting that press as a success: against a stub display
    it moved one setting - the name the panel draws - and no pixels, because
    the picture comes from a file and the edit had never left the buffer.
    "making changes to alpha_new and pressing save as doesn't seem to have any
    effect" was a refusal read as a save: `themes.new_name` answered
    `'alpha_new' is not a free name`, no file changed, and the apply that
    followed sent the name that panel already held.  The press now paints both
    frames - the buffer's, and the one that name will produce - at one body and
    one instant, and says how many px apart they are **before** the command
    goes; a generated name is described as the picture `render.py` draws, not
    as a path that does not exist.  The button is labelled `copy to a new name`
    and its tooltip states the refusal, and the state line reads `edits in this
    buffer only: no file has them, so no display can draw them`.  Twelve
    checks in `tests/test_apply_layout.py`, four of them new; ten ways to break
    it, each counted, in docs/theme-editor.md.

  * The README's advanced-mode picture is remade, because the row that changed
