    it (`diff -rq` with `__pycache__` excluded, one hash over the `.py` list).

  * `Pump Speed` went from a sentence to a rule, because the machine changed
    and the refusal turned out to be unconditional.  On 2026-11-13 the owner
    re-wired this box: six case fans to the cooler's own ARGB controller
    (which exposes no sensor interface to Linux - 12 hwmon chips before, the
    same 12 after), the AIO's block cable to `PUMP_SYS1`, its radiator fans
    to `CPU_FAN1`.  A tach input that had read 0 in all 30 samples of an
    earlier watch came up at 2443-2469 RPM: spread 10 across 23 rounds with
    all 24 threads loaded and spread 5 across 15 at rest, flat while its own
    header's duty matched `pwm1` sample for sample (204-231 in one window,
    152-182 in another) - which is how a pump tells itself from a fan on a
    board that gangs the two headers.  `sensors.FAN_CHANNELS` pins that
    channel as `("CPU PUMP", "Pump")`, and `themes.FAN_TYPED` answers the
    vendor's own `waterPumpSpeed` / `Pump Speed` name from a fan row's type:
    nine layouts, one widget each, +1 box each and 563 px at size 20 or
    1181 px at size 32 on this bench at the pinned 2453 RPM value (the check
    prints those figures rather than gating them).  What was wrong was not
    the missing sensor: `themes.UNMAPPED` was a constant applied last, so a
    body that *did* report a pump row was refused anyway - measured.  The
    refusal is kept for a box with no such row, and the sentence now names
    the command that would settle it (`tools/fanscan.py`, and the pin table)
    instead of asserting a fact about whatever box built the package.
    `render._tach` stops taking a pump for the CPU fan (its pinned name
    contains "cpu", and a pump-only body printed `CPU FAN 2410 RPM`), and
    `--list-themes` asks the reading door rather than a constant, so it
    stops claiming the gap here and keeps saying why on a machine that has
    it.  Two things learned about `/etc/sensors.d` on the way, recorded in
    docs/usage.md: `nct6683` publishes no `fan*_label`, so a label written
    there never reaches sysfs and cannot reach this program; and lm-sensors
    halves a `pwmN` before printing it, so raw 174 reads `87%` and the true
    68% needs `compute pwmN @/1.275`.  Eleven checks in
    `tests/test_pump_speed.py`, seven mutations counted in docs/theme-editor.md
    (restoring the blanket refusal costs six of them); `--watch` now samples the
    `pwm*` outputs in the same window so the pairing is on the record.  And the
    name being in the vocabulary makes it a legal `xigma.showIf` dependency, so
    a row can be gated on a pump being sensed at all: 13 boxes against 12 on
    this bench, the silenced row reported as `showIf:Pump Speed` rather than
    drawn as a hole.

  * Two fields were answering from the wrong place, and both are rules now.
    `sensors.motherboard()` returned 45-50 °C from `r8169` - a Realtek NIC's
    die - because the code took `max()` over a flat list of chip prefixes and
    this board's Super-IO was not in that list at all: its diode and three
    thermistors read 40.5-49.0 while its hottest channel, `AMD TSI Addr 98h`,
    is the CPU at 62-70, so adding the chip naively would only have moved the
    error the other way.  It walks three tiers now - Super-IO, then the DDR5
    SPD thermistors, then the network die as a last resort - and drops a
    channel whose own driver label names a processor or a card (`nct6683`
    labels its temperatures even though it labels no tachometer, and that label
    is the whole discriminator).  Here that answers 49 °C from `nct6687
    [Thermistor 15]` with the NIC sitting at 50.5: the row is no longer the
    box's maximum, and the vendor's own captured body has the same shape
    (`motherboard:38` beside `cpu:57`). `sensors.disk()` took `min()` over
    every `temp*_input` of every disk chip - 31-33 °C here, which was the
    composite of a drive that holds nothing the reader is being told about, the
    capacity numbers coming from the one mounted at `/`.  The rule is that
    drive's `Composite` (46 °C today), its hottest sensor when it publishes no
    composite (55 in the built case), else the hottest across the drives that
    do match when the mount point cannot be read.  The same drive carries raw
    probes at 61.9 and 67.8 which its own aggregate excludes, and the row
    prints the aggregate: quoting 68 would attribute to the drive a reading the
    drive does not treat as its temperature, in a row with no room to say which
    is which.  A new `source` name, `Disk Temperature`, is what lets a layout
    draw it at all - of the 21 distinct `source` values across the 28
    layouts (23 selectable files + 5 generated exports) not one is
    storage-shaped, and docs/theme-editor.md spells out the counting rule
    behind that figure, because the "30" this entry first quoted was
    neither count it could have been.  `hwmon()` also sorted device
    directories as text, so `hwmon10` walked before `hwmon2`
    also sorted device directories as text, so `hwmon10` walked before `hwmon2`
    - harmless while one chip publishes tachometers, and the thing that decides
    which row becomes `Fan 1` the day two do.  `tools/fanscan.py` prints the
    tiers, the channel that won, the labels refused and each drive's composite
    against its raw probes, because every number above belongs to this bench
    and can be reprinted on yours.  Twenty-three checks in
    `tests/test_sensor_rules.py` run against a **built** tree, since a CI
    runner has no NCT6687D and no SPD hubs: the rules are about selection, not
    about what one machine happens to expose.  The fifteen mutations aimed at
    those doors now cost: the flat `max()` back → 5; the label filter gone → 3;
    the NIC promoted into the first tier → 1; the coolest number back → 6; the
    mounted drive ignored → 5; the composite rule dropped → 7; the fallback
    cooled to a minimum → 1; the text sort back → 1; the source name removed →
    2; its display shape removed → 3 (one of those three is
    `tests/test_bars_rows.py`, which holds that every answerable source has a
    shape); the diode preference dropped → 4; the drive list left in walk order
    → 2; the per-drive list re-deriving its own number → 4; an empty disk slot
    going unexplained → 1; the `drives` section kept off the wire while the
    slots still read it → 1.

    Two of those were measured because a mutation came back **free**.  Promoting
    the NIC into the first tier cost nothing once the diode rule existed - the
    diode wins the tier either way on the built tree - so the suite stayed green
    over a rule change that would matter on any board whose Super-IO publishes
    no diode-labelled channel.  That is a hole in the check, not a fact about
    the code, so the check now disables `sensors.DIODE` for one assertion and
    asks the tier again: with no diode to prefer, the Super-IO's thermistor must
    still beat the NIC's 50. It also asserts `r8169` appears in no tier but
    the last. The stale needle on the first mutation (found 0 times) was
    reported out loud by the driver rather than passing silently, which is the
    only reason either gap was visible at all.

    The suite then caught a fault in the new checks themselves, on a machine
    that was not this one: CI #190 came back red (`510 tests in 351.145 s`, one
    failure, `55 != 42`) while this bench was green.  The blob check had let
    `collect()` ask **the host's own mount table** about a synthetic tree, and
    no runner mounts `/` on a drive named as `nvme0` is named here, so the rule
    fell back to its hottest reading and the pinned number was wrong.  The rule
    was right and the check was testing the machine it ran on.  It pins the
    root drive for the built tree now, and a second check asserts the relation
    on the real machine - `collect()` and `disk_temperature()` may not disagree
    about the same number - which is the kind of gate that would have failed
    here too had the two ever drifted.  Re-running the module with the mount
    stubbed to `md0`, a name no drive in the built tree matches, passes all
    fifteen checks; the first version did not, and the runner's own log stands
    as that experiment's record rather than something I re-ran by hand.

    The lesson did not end with that fix.  The relation check written to
    replace the pinned number still assumed the key existed, and CI #191 - on
    the commit meant to fix #190 - errored with `KeyError: 'temperature'`,
    because that runner's VM has a disk chip publishing no temperature at all.
    #192 then came back green over 525 checks, which proved nothing: a
    different image, and the case was simply not present.  I had read the
    run-list column instead of the log and called the fix verified on that
    basis, and that is the part worth keeping: a green run that never reaches
    the failing shape is not evidence.  The check now branches on the absent
    case, and a constructed one - `HWMON_GLOB` pointed at an empty tree -
    drives it on every machine: `disk_temperature()` answers None with the
    no-disk-chip sentence, the blob carries no `temperature` key, no
    `motherboard` section and no `fans` key, and `motherboard_answer()` and
    `fans()` answer nothing.  Making even that right cost a fourth fault of the
    same family: the first version of the corrected check compared two live
    reads of this box and failed here with `38 != 39`, because the drive's
    composite moved a whole degree between the calls - a check asking whether
    two doors share a rule, answering a question about how fast a drive warms
    up.  The reads are snapshotted for the duration now: one view of sysfs, two
    doors compared.

    Five checks in one session that were wrong about the machine rather than
    the code: a mount table that names `nvme0` (#190); a call named in a file
    being a call that runs, where `if not a.skip_frame:` -> `if False:` kept the
    name and passed the check; a `disk` section implying a `temperature` key
    (#191); a drive whose temperature holds still for the length of a function
    call (`38 != 39`); and, an hour after that one was written up, the same
    fault re-committed in brand-new code - a check comparing two live reads of
    `sensors.drives()`, green standalone and red in the full suite forty
    seconds later with `[44, 59, 68]` against `[44, 58, 68]`.  The repair was
    the same each time - pin or construct the inputs, and assert a relation
    instead of a reading - and repeating it anyway is the reason the pattern is
    written out here rather than left as four separate fixes.  Two more
    mutations counted: `collect()` bypassing the disk rule -> 2; the blob
    inventing a temperature where nothing answers -> 1.  The sensor mutations
    were re-run twice more after that, and the second re-run is the one whose
    counts this entry quotes: they moved because the check set grew, not because
    the rules did.

  * A tool to ask the panel the two questions the last cycles left unmeasured,
    and eighteen checks that gate it without touching a port.  Since the pump
    rule the tree sends `"type":"Pump"` in the metrics blob - a value none of
    the 34 captured `fans[]` entries spells - and per-drive disk rows would
    need a `drives` section, a key the vendor's schema does not have.
    PROTOCOL.md §8 records that the panel answers a bare `200` to
    `STATE all 1`, which is not the same statement as "it answers `200` to a
    body with a key it has never seen".  `tools/blob_probe.py` sends the
    current body as a control, then the Pump-typed one, then the extra-section
    one, then both, then the control again, and uploads one ordinary frame at
    the end, because five `200`s mean nothing if the next picture is refused.
    Without `--yes` it prints the plan - bodies, deltas and the frame control -
    and opens nothing; it refuses outright while the display service holds the
    port (one opener, §1), gated by stubbing that answer so the check means
    the same on a runner with no panel; it never prints the panel's serial
    (`PRINTED_KEYS` has no `sn`); and an AST walk over the tool's own source
    asserts that no string reaching a `request`/`post` call is a destructive
    or firmware topic and that the only verbs used are `STATE all 1`, `conn`,
    `hello`.  The five bodies are checked as data too: the pump body differs
    from the control by exactly one row and one value with no new top-level
    key, the `drives` body by exactly one key, and the second control equals
    the first - so the two questions cannot be answered at once and mean
    nothing.  Nine mutations, each counted: the `--yes` guard removed -> 3;
    the service check removed -> 1; the serial admitted -> 1; a `recovery`
    post inserted -> 2; the pump body grown with a top-level key -> 1; the
    control keeping the `Pump` type it is meant to isolate -> 2; the control
    keeping the `drives` section the experiment adds -> 2; the probe building
    its own drive rows instead of calling `sensors.drives()` -> 3; the end
    frame control switched off -> 1.  Two of those checks exist only because
    the mutation they now catch came back **free** the first time.  Turning
    the frame control off (`if not a.skip_frame:` -> `if False:`) kept the
    name `upload_a_frame` in the file and passed a check that asked only
    whether that name appeared.  Binding the check to the guard fixed that,
    and the fix then failed against the unmutated tool, because the guard
    spells `skip_frame` as an attribute of `a` rather than a bare name - the
    baseline caught my own AST walk being wrong, the same lesson as the
    previous entry, twelve minutes later.  The other one was committed
    against real hardware before it was caught: replacing `drive_rows()` with
    a hand-built list passed every data check in the file, and the second
    probe run had in fact sent such a list - whole degrees for the row,
    `0.1` degrees in its `sensors` list, rows left in walk order - while the
    shipped door rounds both and sorts by controller.  So the probe is now
    bound to the thing it certifies: one check reads this tool's AST for the
    call to `sensors.drives` and refuses the moment it reaches for
    `_disk_channels` again, and another asserts the rows equal
    `sensors.drives()` on whatever machine runs it and agree with what the
    `Disk 1` slots read.  Also: the new test file leaked two handles through
    `io.open(...).read()` - the noise this cycle removed elsewhere - now
    `with`-guarded, and the module runs clean under `-W error::ResourceWarning`.

    Binding the probe to the door took two tries, and the first attempt made
    the mistake this session has now written down four times.  The check
    compared `drive_rows()` with `sensors.drives()` on the machine running it,
    passed standalone, and failed in the full suite about forty seconds later:
    `[44, 59, 68]` against `[44, 58, 68]`.  Two reads of sysfs, a few hundred
    milliseconds apart, and the drive warmed a degree between them - so the
    check was measuring how fast an NVMe drive heats up, again, in code whose
    whole point was to stop that happening.  It is rewritten around a
    **constructed** tree now, which also makes it non-vacuous: the built
    `nvme2` sits in `hwmon7`, first in walk order at 55 degrees, while the
    controller order puts it third, so a `drive_rows()` that re-derived rows
    from the walk - the payload the second probe run really sent - fails
    there.  The live relation is kept as well, with `sensors._read` cached so
    one snapshot answers both doors.
    `-W error::ResourceWarning`.

    It then ran on this box - display service stopped, probe, service
    restarted - and the answers came back uniform: five `STATE all 1`
    bodies, each `<Message 1 200 {AckNumber, CkOK:True} b''>`, and the
    ordinary `.osd` frame uploaded after them accepted, `space` and
    `osdState`/`mode` unchanged.  The panel does not reject a metrics
    body for a value or a key it has never seen, so per-drive disk rows
    are a layout question rather than a protocol one.  Two of those runs
    were wrong about their own payloads, and both transcripts are kept.
    `183954` took its control straight from `sensors.collect()`, which on this
    box already reports the pinned pump channel, so control and experiment both
    carried the `Pump` value and that pair distinguished nothing - its `drives`
    experiment was a genuine new key that day, because `collect()` had no such
    section.  `184215` fixed the Pump pairing but sent a `drives` payload the
    probe had assembled itself, `0.1` degrees and unsorted, rather than the
    `sensors.drives()` that shipped later the same evening, so it certified a
    near-miss of the body that goes out.  A third fault of the same family -
    `base = dict(raw)`, a control carrying `drives` once `collect()` had grown
    the section - was caught by `tests/test_blob_probe.py` before it ever
    reached the panel.
    The run that counts is `2026-11-13-223617-blob-probe.txt`: the
    control stripped of both values, the `drives` payload produced by the
    shipped door, an 11 668 B frame accepted afterwards and `space`
    `68140 -> 68140`.  In the two flawed runs the acks printed were the
    same `200`s; the *question* was not the one claimed.  What the
    standing run still cannot show is whether the panel *reads* those
    fields - a `STATE` reply carries no body, `space` does not move for
    accepted uploads, and the only topic that would make the panel draw
    the numbers itself is `realtimeDisplay`, which ALPHA sends only when
    `deviceConfig.OS` is `RTOS` while this unit reports Linux.  One side
    effect written down instead of dropped: after the first probe the
    restarted loop's session-start background upload was refused once and
    retried (18:40:07) - the same unexplained single-`400` class this tree
    logs elsewhere, arriving immediately after a probe, and not reproduced
    on purpose because that would mean taking the port again.  All three
    transcripts are unpublished and none carries the panel's serial.

  * The board's own label decides which Super-IO channel answers, and the
    drives get slots of their own.  Both follow the owner's rulings of
    2026-11-13, and both are recorded as rules rather than as numbers.

    `Motherboard Temperature`: within whichever tier answers, a channel the
    driver labels a **diode** now beats that tier's hottest candidate.  The
    BIOS and the owner's `/etc/sensors.d` file both call `Diode 0 (curr)`
    "Motherboard", so the row follows what the machine labels rather than the
    maximum it could find: measured side by side here, diode 41.5 degrees,
    thermistors 43.0, so the glass prints 42 where the previous rule printed
    43 - a degree of headroom given up to agree with the board.  A chip that
    publishes no diode-labelled channel, which is most Super-IOs under a driver
    that leaves `tempN_label` unset, still falls back to its hottest candidate.
    The built tree in `tests/test_sensor_rules.py` sets the diode deliberately
    cooler than the thermistors so the preference cannot pass by accident, one
    check asserts the winning label contains "diode", and a newer check
    disables the preference to prove the tiers still matter without it.

    Per-drive rows: `sensors.drives()` publishes a `drives` section in the blob
    - `{name, temperature, sensors}` per storage controller, ordered by
    controller so `nvme0` is slot 1 whatever the kernel numbered its hwmon
    directory - and `themes.DISK_SLOTS` names eight more sources for layouts:
    `Disk 1 Temperature` .. `Disk 4 Temperature` and their `Disk N Name`
    partners, with the source vocabulary at 37 names and the editor's `source`
    and `showIf` menus along with it.  Measured here: `nvme0` 45 degrees with
    published sensors [45, 57, 68] and `nvme1` 33, so the boot drive goes in
    slot 1 and the hotter raw probe stays visible in the row instead of being
    dropped; an empty slot is reported, in words, like every other hole.  Which
    reading of a drive is "its temperature" is now one function,
    `_drive_choice()`, shared by the scalar and the list: three copies of that
    rule had appeared while this was being written, which is the drift this
    repository keeps having to hunt for.  `tools/fanscan.py` prints the list in
    slot order next to each drive's composite and raw probes, and no shipped
    layout asks for any of these names - the vendor's library has no storage
    metric at all - so the `bars` parity figures and the library's drawn-box
    counts are unchanged.  `tests/test_sensor_rules.py` is twenty-three
    checks and `tests/test_blob_probe.py` eighteen; their censuses are below.

  * These rows are on the wire because the panel was asked, and asked again
    after the payload changed.  The first probe run that certified a `drives`
    section had sent a list the tool assembled itself, not the shipped one, so
    the experiment was re-run once `sensors.drives()` existed: five bodies,
    every one answered `1 200` with `CkOK True`, an 11 668 B frame accepted
    afterwards and `space` `68140 -> 68140`
    (`data/glass/2026-11-13-223617-blob-probe.txt`, PROTOCOL.md §8).  What was
    *not* won by that run is stated there too - no `STATE` reply carries a
    body, so nothing shows whether the panel reads the section, only that it
    does not refuse it.

  * A sixth machine-dependence, found because the runner contradicted itself
    rather than because this bench did.  `tests/test_blob_probe.py` had a check
    asserting its per-drive list was non-empty - a fact about the host, not
    the code.  CI #193 ran 526 checks green in 431.661 s; CI #194 ran the
    **same 526 checks** to one failure in 460.878 s (`[] is not true : this
    machine publishes no disk temperatures at all`), and the whole difference
    between those two commits is eight lines of this file's prose (`git diff
    --stat e69c5d7 61b1d6a`: one file).  So the green run had proved nothing
    and the red run had proved nothing, in both directions: some runner images
    publish a disk temperature chip and some do not.  The check now runs
    against the built sysfs in `tests/test_sensor_rules.py`, whose three
    drives are deliberately ordered one way by walk number and another way by
    controller, so a list re-derived from the walk still fails there.

    `tools/audit_bare_runner.py` turns that class of fault into a one-minute
    measurement instead of a review: it points `sensors.HWMON_GLOB` at a
    pattern matching no directory - a box with no hardware-monitoring chips at
    all, stricter than any runner - and re-runs every test module that names a
    live sensor door.  The list is **derived from each file's AST**, not grep:
    a docstring quoting `sensors.collect()` is not a reader, while
    `sensors.collect = lambda: body` is one, because that assignment is a
    module admitting it would otherwise touch the machine.  Only the case the
    AST cannot see - a test that runs a shipped CLI in a subprocess - is kept
    by hand.  Here that reported 95 checks over 6 modules, 0 failures, 0
    errors, with `0 chips visible, 0 fan rows, motherboard() -> {},
    drives() -> []` - the count is the tool's to print, not these pages'
    to keep, and it went to 96 one commit later when a check was added to
    `tests/test_sensor_rules.py`.  docs/tools.md and docs/theme-editor.md name
    the command rather than the number for that reason.

    Ten checks in `tests/test_bare_audit.py` hold the tool's two promises, and
    the census after them: the substitution pointed back at the real tree ->
    2; the derivation finding nothing -> 4; the finder degraded to a text grep
    -> 2; the `finally`-restore made a no-op -> 1; a derived module also
    listed by hand -> 1; the tool not reporting what it substituted -> 1.  The
    fourth of those read `0 failures` the first time it was run, and that
    reading was its own trap: the mutation did not parse, so the test module
    failed to import and the driver counted a run that had not happened.  The
    driver now parses before it trusts a number and prints NOT MEASURED if it
    cannot, and the mutation was rewritten to be valid.  A census that cannot
    tell you it measured nothing is not a census - which is the same lesson as
    a green CI run that never reaches the failing shape, arriving in the tool
    built to look for it.



  * Which on-board silicon may answer a `Motherboard` row, decided from a
    measured map of the box rather than from a datasheet.  This boot exposes
    **22 temperature channels across 8 chips**: the Super-IO's diode at 38.5
    and its three thermistors at 39.0-39.5 (plus `AMD TSI Addr 98h` at 60.0,
    which is the CPU), the four DDR5 SPD hubs at 36.8-38.2, `r8169` at 43.5,
    `prom21_xhci` - the X870's Promontory hub die, soldered down - at
    49.2-50.1, the `ath12k_hwmon` Wi-Fi module at 42.0, `amdgpu` at 42.0,
    `k10temp` 42.1-60.9 and two NVMe drives up to 67.8.  On the owner's
    ruling, the hub die joins the **last-resort** tier: when a box has
    neither a Super-IO diode nor an SPD hub, a chipset hub is a better
    stand-in for "the board" than a network controller is.  The Wi-Fi module
    joins **no** tier, because a module on a socket is a card in every sense
    the row is meant to exclude and nothing measured here could tell it apart
    from an add-in card's die.
    On this machine the first ruling changes nothing at all - the diode wins
    tier 1 either way - and that is said plainly rather than dressed up as an
    improvement: it is a rule for a box this project has not seen, and it is
    checked on a constructed tree where the hub die sits a hair above the NIC
    so the tier's own ordering is visible, and taking the hub back out gives
    the NIC the tier again.
    The second ruling needed a guard that is not a label match, and that is
    the part worth the code.  `ath12k_hwmon` and `nvme` publish channels
    labelled plain `temp1`/`Composite`, which the existing label filter
    (`TSI|Tctl|Tccd|CPU|GPU|edge|hotspot`) would accept the day a tier named
    them.  So `NOT_THE_BOARD_CHIPS` skips whole chips by name, and the check
    proves it the only way a membership rule can be proved: the built tree
    gives the Wi-Fi die **61 °C**, hotter than every board candidate, and
    asks for it as a tier - the answer must be nothing.  A rule that only
    rejects things by their name is one typo away from printing an SSD as the
    motherboard.  `tools/fanscan.py` now lists the chips it found and refused
    by name, so this is re-runnable on any box: here it prints `amdgpu,
    ath12k_hwmon, k10temp, nvme`.

