  * The boot drive gets a row of its own in `bars`, and its price was
    measured before it was paid.  `themes.BARS_DISK_ROWS` is one labelled row -
    `DISK`, number and meter both on `disk.temperature`, the same scalar that
    describes the capacity printed beside it - sitting after `BOARD` so the
    component temperatures keep together: CPU, GPU, BOARD, DISK, LOAD, RAM,
    VRAM, then the four fan slots.  Labelling it `Disk 1 Name` would have
    paired a positional name with a non-positional reading in a single row,
    which is how a row starts meaning two different drives on two different
    boxes.
    What one more row costs is arithmetic: the pitch is `BARS_SPAN //
    len(rows)`, so at span 320 ten rows gave 32 px and eleven give 29 - and
    at 29 the shipped meter (`BARS_BAR_DY` 12 over a 6 px track) sat **1 px**
    from the text band of the row below it, nearer that row's label (1 px)
    than its own (3 px).  Both halves of the clearance check failed, and they
    are the check that exists because a 10 px track did exactly this in
    2026-11-12.  Two ways out were swept on the shipped painter, measuring
    ink rather than reasoning about it: thin the meter to (11, 4), which
    clears 2 px above and 4 below - at the floor, on a track already thinned
    once for this same reason; or give the block the room under it, which is
    empty: the last row's meter ends at y=409 and the clock line's own ink
    begins at y=440, 31 px of measured air on a 480 px panel.  `BARS_SPAN`
    went 320 -> 352, the pitch stayed 32, the track stayed 6 px, and the
    clearances became 3 above / 4 below.
    After: `bars` is 35 widgets, 29 rows drawn at the editor's pinned fixture
    with 6 reported skipped (the `Fan 3`/`Fan 4` slots, same in kind as before),
    and `themes.parity("bars", edit.FIXTURE, edit.FIXTURE_WHEN)` reports 0
    differing pixels, worst channel 0, 57 036 lit px down each door.  The row
    census `tests/test_row_notes.py` now prints moved 145 -> 147 `text`/`data`
    rows over 28 layouts with 134 twins and 13 skips; the fixture answers 16 of
    the 20 sources the five exports ask, leaving exactly the four fan slots; and
    the 23 pickable library files still paint 114 boxes and 315 287 lit px,
    because the row went into a generated layout rather than a vendored one.
    `edit.FIXTURE` grew a `disk` section so the preview answers the row - a pane
    that silences a reading this box reports is the half-truth that fixture was
    completed to stop being. `fixtures.PARITY_CASES` grew a
    `disk.temperature`: mid-scale 41 in the first complete case, the top of
    the 25..80 meter (80) in the second where every other field sits at an
    end of its own scale. That went in because the parity check reported
    `['Disk Temperature', 'Disk Temperature']` - number and meter, from both
    doors - and was right to.
    `tests/test_recorded_series.py` broke first, and informatively: it sliced
    `BARS_ROWS[len(fixed):]` and called everything past the six fixed rows a
    fan slot, so the new row reached `int('Temperature')` - an assumption about
    where rows live, checked by counting rather than by asking what they are.
    It splits the table by shape now, and the published series answers the row:
    all twelve samples carry a `disk.temperature` of 33, inside the 25..80
    meter, so the tracked clip's provenance covers the reading instead of
    quietly not containing it.
    `--check-theme bars` lists `Disk Temperature`
    among the shapes this repo chose rather than measured off the vendor's
    picture, as it already did for `Time` and the fan slots.
    Eight mutations, counted as failing check-runs (the parity probe repeats per
    reading): the table loses the row while the code still draws it -> 26; the
    code door loses it while the table declares it -> 28; the two doors read
    different fields for one row -> 14; the meter's scale moves in the table
    only -> 8; the span goes back to 320 with eleven rows -> 12; the label
    follows the reading instead of the subject in one door -> 5; the row moves
    to the end of the table alone -> 21; the row left out of the pitch
    arithmetic -> 21.
    Three positional lookups in `tests/test_bars_rows.py` (row 4 meant RAM,
    row 5 meant VRAM) became `slot_of("RAM")` reads against the table.
    Inserting a row above a hard-coded index silently re-points that check at
    a different row - it passes, and measures the wrong thing - which is the
    same fault shape as a count that stops matching the tree it came from.

