  * `bars` now shows both NVMe drives, and the gigabyte rows stop flooring.
    Two things the owner asked for in one sitting, both measured before and
    after.

    **The drive rows.** The single `DISK` row is gone; `bars` reserves **two
    positional drive slots** beside the four fan slots - `Disk 1 Name` /
    `Disk 1 Temperature` and `Disk 2 ...` - so the block is twelve rows on
    every machine and the vendored row count stops being a fact about one
    box. Ordering changed to make that honest: `sensors.drives()` puts **the
    drive under `/` first**, then the rest by controller, so slot 1 is by
    rule the same drive whose `disk.total` / `disk.used` the blob reports,
    and the boot drive can be marked by position -
    `layout.VENDOR_FORMAT["Disk 1 Name"]` prints `nvme0 /`, the other slots
    plain.  A check proves the ordering rather than the box: the built tree's
    controllers are `nvme0`, `nvme1`, `nvme2` in name order, so pointing
    the mount at `nvme1` is what separates "root first" from "alphabetical",
    and slot 1 must equal the scalar at both mounts.  On the shipped painter
    at this machine's own readings the block now writes `CPU 61°C`, `GPU 44°C`,
    `BOARD 39°C`, `nvme0 / 40°C`, `nvme1 29°C`, `LOAD 1%`, `RAM 82.8 GB`,
    `VRAM 15.6 GB`, `Fan CPU 1626` and `CPU PUMP 2453`, with the two fan slots
    reported unanswered.
    The geometry the owner's eye caught and my arithmetic missed: the page
    said 31 px of air under the
    block, but the block itself carries blank reserved slots, so the real gap
    measured **93 px** between the last row's ink (y=335) and the clock's ink
    (y=445) - room for roughly three rows, exactly as seen on the glass.  So
    the span grew to 384 and the clock moved from y=452 to y=458, and the
    pitch stayed 32 px with the 6 px track untouched.  Twelve rows is now the
    ceiling the reservation implies, and a new check measures the margin in
    the case never drawn here - every slot answered - holding the last row's
    meter (to y=442) clear of the clock's ink (from y=446) with a floor of 2
    px, and printing what a thirteenth row would compute (pitch 29, which the
    clearance rule already refuses).

    **The gigabyte rows.** `VRAM` read `15 GB` on a card whose meter stood at
    **97.4%**: 15 952 MB of 16 376 is 15.58 GiB, and the number's rule was
    `int(mb) // 1024`, so one row showed a nearly full card as having a
    gigabyte of headroom.  Rounding to whole gigabytes is not better - it
    prints `16 GB`, equal to the total, at 97% - so the spelling is now one
    decimal, `15.6 GB`, from one function `render.gb()` that
    `layout.VENDOR_FORMAT` references for the file door and `theme_bars`
    calls for the code door.  The two gigabyte rows share it instead of each
    carrying a lambda of its own, which a row-string check now pins, and a
    new check holds a row's two halves to the same fraction (printed value
    over the card's total within 0.01 of the meter) while asserting the floor
    rule **would** have disagreed by more than 0.03 - so the guard fails if
    someone decides a whole gigabyte looks tidier. Width costs nothing: the
    widest number in the block, `82.8 GB`, starts at x=365 and the label
    column ends by x=133.

    Three faults of mine surfaced while doing it, recorded rather than
    smoothed over.  The first version of the slot values put `None` in the
    table for an unanswered slot, and `layout.draw_bar` then raised
    `float() argument must be a string or a real number, not 'NoneType'` -
    the fan slots already had the right rule (omit the key, report it in
    `absent` ), and mine was a second copy written badly. Replacing `fmt`
    with `gb` on the RAM/VRAM rows silently fed the formatter the meter's
    **fraction** instead of the used field, printing `0.1 GB` for 10 240 MB;
    the parity gate caught it as an 831 px split on the first reading with a
    GPU. And `tests/test_bars_rows.py`'s `wanted_rows()` had rebuilt the
    slot-name map from `FAN_SLOTS` and counted `metrics["fans"]` by hand - a
    second copy of the rule, which raised `KeyError: 'Disk 1 Temperature'`
    the day the drive slots arrived.  Both doors and the tests now read one
    map, `themes.SLOT_NAMES` .

    Census and coverage: `tests/test_bars_rows.py` 24 -> 28 (a `TheSlotRows`
    class for a drive row with a name and no reading, and
    `TheBlockStopsBeforeTheClock` for the margin),
    `tests/test_sensor_rules.py` 23 -> 24 (root-first ordering), and
