    Also found, unrelated to the request and worth its own line:
    `xigmalcd/sensors.py` still carried a **second, dead** `BOARD_CHIPS`
    assignment from before the tier rules - defined at the top of the file,
    overridden 50 lines later, and harmless only because Python takes the
    last binding.  It is gone, and one definition of each chip list is now
    what the module has.

  * A cancelled CI run, what it actually measured, and two rules taken from
    it.  CI #202 attempt 1 was cancelled: the checks job ran 1816 s against
    its 30-minute cap, and its `tests/` step alone took **1762 s** where the
    same work took 406, 406, 412 and 415 s in the four neighbouring runs -
    about 4.2x slower, with no single stall longer than 25 s anywhere in the
    step (measured by scanning the log's inter-line gaps), i.e. a uniformly
    loaded runner rather than one hung test.  One GUI check failed under that
    load.  Attempt 2, same commit, came back green: **555 checks in
    414.204 s**, skipped=4, all jobs success.

    Two responses, both measured rather than shrugged at.

    *The check that failed was making a bet on the scheduler.* The window-
    sizing suite waits after a resize with `settle(passes)`, which pumps the
    Qt event loop for a fixed wall-clock budget and then asserts geometry.  On
    this bench the state it waits for settles in **3 pumps (0.09 s)** - printed
    now by a new check, because that number was previously an assumption - so
    the fixed bet looked safe and only fails on a machine that slow.  Four
    resize-dependent checks now wait on the **state** instead
    (`settle_until_stable`, which samples until the splitter or the field grid
    stops changing, bounded at 60 pumps, and returns what it cost), and the new
    check asserts the bound is met and the sampler actually converged - an
    unbounded wait is as useless as a fixed one.

    *The budget was one bad runner wide.* 30 minutes was described in the
    workflow as "four times what the job takes", and the observed worst was
    1816 s: four times, with 2% to spare.  The checks job is capped at **45
    minutes** now, with those figures in the comment above it, and
    `tests/test_ci_workflow.py` (four checks, new) reads the workflow by
    indentation - no YAML dependency, because pyyaml is not one this project
    declares - and holds three things: every job declares a timeout at all,
    the checks budget covers the observed worst without becoming permission to
    hang, and the comment beside the number still carries the measurements.
    Four mutations counted: the deb job's timeout removed -> 1; the checks
    budget tidied back to 30 -> 1; the figures stripped from the comment -> 1;
    a job added without a timeout -> 2.  The first attempt at that census
    reported **0 failures**, and the cause was the driver, not the code: the
    needle did not match the file, so the mutation never happened.  The driver
    now asserts each needle occurs exactly once before trusting a count.

    What is **not** claimed: a cause for the slow runner, or a leak.  The log's
    cleanup line - `Terminate orphan process: pid (3837) (python3)` and
    `(16141) (ffmpeg)` - is what cancellation looks like when the suite is
    killed mid-decode: every `ffmpeg` call in this codebase goes through
    `subprocess.run`, which waits, so a live ffmpeg at that moment is a child
    of the still-live suite, not a runaway.  Why that runner was 4.2x slow is
    unknown and stays unknown; what was in reach was the flake it exposed and
    the margin that turned a slow machine into a red tick.  Worth noting for
    scale: the window's own thumbnail pass is a documented cost - 115 clips,
    115 `ffmpeg` seeks, **12.7 s** to populate, recorded in `app.py` - so the
    suite's GUI checks carry real per-window work, and a contended runner pays
    for all of it in series.

  * A gutter of figures beside the rows: drawn vectors, named from the
    layout, coloured by the row's own reading.

    The owner asked for intuitive icons - CPU, GPU, fan, pump, board,
    RAM/VRAM, storage - and offered SVGs, Font Awesome, Nerd Fonts or any
    mix as means. What was built is none of those, for two measured
    reasons. A glyph from an icon **font** resolves against whatever face
    this machine has installed, and this repository has already paid for
    face dependence twice: the vendor's own `simsun` rows are *dropped* on
    a runner with no CJK face (docs/limits.md, and
    `tests/test_library_draw.py` reports the faces a machine cannot
    resolve), and the same `text` row measures different ink widths under
    Liberation Sans than under the Arial the vendor designed for. A figure
    that vanishes because the host lacks a font is worse than no figure,
    and a figure that changes width with the host breaks the rule this
    project holds highest: both doors paint the same pixels. A **baked
    asset** - SVG rasterised to a PNG - fails the other way: it cannot be
    moved, resized or recoloured from a layout, it puts a second copy of
    the theme's ink outside the palette rules, and `--check-theme` could
    not tell a reader which of its parts came from where.

    So the figures are polygons, drawn in `render.ICONS` , and a layout
    names one through the `special` kind that already exists:
    `name: "icon-chip4"` , `x` / `y` the box corner, `width` / `height` its
    size, `source` / `minValue` / `maxValue` the reading, and
    `xigma.colourRamp: "heat"` . That is the fourth door for a `special` -
    `layout.draw_special_glyph` - joining `line` , `pair` and `values` ,
    and the door-marker check in `tests/test_specials.py` was extended to
    hold it to the same rule: exactly one door per registered drawing,
    marked, and the marker verified against the call the body actually
    makes.

    Nine figures, each chosen for what survives at **22 px** (one
    twenty-second of the glass, which is a hard design limit: at that size
    a second detail becomes mud): `chip4` a die with leads on four edges
    and a solid core (CPU), `card` a plate with a bracket tab and a blower
    ring (GPU), `board` the plate with a socket, two expansion slots and
    four mounting holes, `drive` an M.2 body with a **keyed end** drawn as
    a step in the outline (both storage rows), `dimm` a stick with four
    chips and a notch in its bottom edge (RAM), `chip2` a package with
    leads on two edges and a pin-1 index dot (VRAM), `fan` a ring with
    three wedges and a hollow hub, `impeller` a volute ring with two broad
    vanes and a **tangential outlet** (the pump), and `gauge` a 240-degree
    dial whose **needle is the reading**. That last one is the creative
    part the others are not: `LOAD` at 3 % points the needle low and at 97
    % points it high, because the angle comes from the same fraction the
    meter beside it fills with.

    Colour is not decoration either: each figure is drawn in `heat(frac)`
    of its own row, so a cold CPU die is cyan-green beside a half-full
    meter and a hot pump volute is red beside a full one. The fraction
    comes from `layout.frac_of` , the same call the row's meter goes
    through, so the two cannot disagree - which is the same defect class as
    the `15 GB` -at-97 % row, one figure and one bar describing one reading
    at two colours. And a figure deliberately carries **no** `textColor` :
    choosing amber for the text must not make a cool chip warm, and a check
    in `tests/test_ink.py` refuses the key on an icon widget while
    asserting the census still adds up (24 glyph roles + 11 meter tracks +
    11 figures = 46 widgets).

    **A pump is chosen by what was measured, never by what a label
    spells.** `xigma. iconVariant: "Fan 2 Type"` names another of the row's
    readings; the value table answers `Pump` because `sensors.FAN_CHANNELS`
    pinned that channel by a tachometer that stays flat while its own
    header's duty sweeps (PROTOCOL.md §8, docs/limits.md); and
    `render.ICON_VARIANTS` maps rotor + `Pump` -> volute. Four new source
    names went into the vocabulary to carry it - `Fan 1 Type` ..
    `Fan 4 Type` , 38 -> 42 through `themes.source_names()` - with a
    display shape each, because a type is a fact the machine reports about
    a channel and a layout may want to print it. The honesty check is
    driven in code, not asserted in prose: a row renamed `CPU PUMP` that
    reports `type: Fan` still draws a rotor, and a row named `Pump` that
    reports `Pump` draws the volute, in both doors. Label-sniffing is how
    this project has been wrong about sensors repeatedly, so the drawing is
    not allowed to do it.

    The gate for all of it is a new file, `tests/test_icons.py` (15
    checks), and it earned its keep before it was finished. Two checks
    caught real geometry bugs in the first cut: the die's leads and the
    stick's ends were drawn **outside** the box they declare - the
    double-scaled arithmetic put a CPU figure's ink at x=27.6 against a
    label column starting at x=28 - and the frame's whole-frame parity
    still reported a plausible number while the two doors disagreed about
    which of them won the overlap (1 720 px of grey against green). So
    `test_ink_stays_inside_the_declared_box` measures the ink of every
    registered figure against its box, and
    `test_the_two_doors_agree_on_the_figure_column` compares the doors **in
    the gutter column, row by row**, because a whole-frame diff can be paid
    for by an offset in one place and still come out even. Two more measure
    the claims themselves: `test_the_volute_is_not_the_rotor` (a measured
    pixel difference, not a rename) and
    `test_the_needle_of_the_usage_dial_is_the_reading` (both doors must
    move when the reading moves).

    **Two coverage holes in the new gate, found the way they keep being
    found here: a mutation that cost nothing.** Twelve were run. The first
    hole: the door resolving a figure's variant from the row's **label**
    rather than its reported type cost **0** - because my honesty check
    compared the two doors only at the body where the type *was* `Pump` ,
    where a label rule and a type rule agree. The case that separates them
    is the awkward one: a row named `CPU PUMP` that reports `type: Fan` .
    Driving both bodies through both doors makes that mutation cost 1. The
    second hole: giving the CPU row the GPU's figure - wrong, and
    consistent across the doors, so parity, the ink box, the column census
    and the clearance checks all stayed silent. Nothing in the suite asked
    whether rows about different things look different. So `bars` now has a
    mapping check of its own: the three temperature rows must wear three
    distinct figures, system memory and card memory two distinct ones, the
    two storage rows one figure each, the four tachometer rows a shared
    rotor, and no figure may be reused across those families - which makes
    the mutation cost 1 and states, in code rather than in prose, what the
    column is *for*. Final counts for the twelve: 2, 1, **1**, 27, **1**,
    1, 13, 1, 16, 5, 3, 1 (the two in bold were the zeros until the checks
    grew).

    And one defect the suite did not find at all: adding a fourth `special`
    door left the caption table behind in `--list-specials` , and the flag
    raised `KeyError: 'glyph'` while **596 checks ran green**. It was found
    by typing the command. That is now a check - the flag is run, every
    registered drawing must appear in its output with the door it claims,
    and an entry with no caption is a failure rather than a hole in a
    printout - because a listing kept *beside* a registry is the same
    second-copy mistake this file has been written to catch in everybody
    else's code. Three findings of process, recorded because they repeat.
    `themes.keys_read()` finds the extension keys the painter reads by
    scanning for lookups hung off `prop` or `ext` - an inline
    `(prop.get("xigma") or {}).get("iconVariant")` on a temporary is
    **invisible** to it, which would have left `xigma.iconVariant` a key
    with a written reason and no reader (fixed by binding `ext` , and the
    reason is stated in the door's own docstring). The door markers use
    `inspect.getsource` + `ast.parse` , which refuses a nested closure's
    indented source - `textwrap.dedent` now. And a check that insists every
    `special` reports a box had to learn the honest exception: a figure
    whose row this body cannot answer draws nothing, and that absence is
    already named by the source in `cannot` , so the check now separates
    "box-less because unanswered" from "box-less and silent", which is the
    defect it was written for.

    Two more pins moved with the row size, both corrected by deriving
    rather than re-typing: `tests/test_row_acts.py`'s reorder test walked
    a **literal** three steps on the theory that a row is label, number,
    meter, and now computes the steps from the export (
    `per_row = (widgets - 2) // rows` , four today); and the generated-file
    check that counted three widgets per row counts four. The block is 46
    widgets (11 rows x 4 plus heading and clock), the row census 147
    text/data rows unchanged, and the snap census 199 rows with **103**
    sharing a column - eleven of those new sharings are the gutter figures,
    all at x=3 - while the gap histogram stayed {0: 103, 2: 12, 3: 4, 4:
    14, 5: 4} with nothing at 1, 6 or 7 px, for the fifth census running.
    The name census moved 108 -> 119 source rows, 190 rows carrying at
    least one.

