    because it changed no count: a prose-only push, and it ran those same 547
    checks in 462.593 s against #197's 403.049 s - 59.544 s apart on trees whose
    only difference was sentences.  #200 then ran the 549 of this commit in
    410.181 s.  #202 ran 555 in 414.204 s, and is in the series for a reason
    the others are not: its **first attempt, on the same commit**, spent 1762 s
    in the `tests/` step and reached 1816 s of job before the 30-minute cap
    cancelled it, against 406, 406, 412 and 415 s for that step in its four
    neighbours - no single gap of 25 s or more anywhere in the log, so a
    uniformly loaded runner rather than one hung test, and the only sample here
    that says something about the machine rather than the tree.  It is kept
    with the failure it induced, not instead of it.  So a pair of these numbers
    can bound a count and nothing else, which is why the list is kept and no
    check reads it - the figures a check *does* read are the ones copied next to
    the budget in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, held there by
    `tests/test_ci_workflow.py`.  #190 came back red
    rather than slow (510 in 351.145 s, one failure, and the failure was a new
    check reading the host it ran on); #191 was red one level down, and #192
    green without ever reaching the shape that broke it.  A sample is not added
    for a prose-only push: the list records what changed a count, not every
    commit.  The 435 in this entry's first bullet is what the port landed at,
    not what the tree holds.

    And how this bullet went wrong once, since the record should say.  While
    these numbers were being set, a helper that re-wraps paragraphs broke a
    quoted command in two places in this same file - `^    def test_` lost its
    four spaces and became `^ def test_`, a command that counts something else
    - and an earlier splice left a second copy of this paragraph standing
    below the first.  The width gate passed on both.  A gate over line lengths
        cannot see that the content of a sentence changed, so
    `tests/test_debian_changelog.py`
    now also refuses any code span that carries a quoted argument across a line
    break.  Run against the whole file, that check found exactly two spans: the
    command above, and a `{"cmd": "text_color", "value": "…"}` example in a
    0.4-era stanza where the wrap had been cosmetic and unread for months.  Both
    are on one line now.


 -- Tamer Hassan <thameruddin@gmail.com>  Wed, 11 Nov 2026 22:05:00 +0200

xigmatek-connect-lcd (0.6) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Released as `v0.6`, the first tag of a tree whose layout editor writes
    files: this stanza is the record of the work since `v0.5`, and the stamp
    at the foot of it is a packaging fact rather than a date -
    `dpkg-buildpackage` takes `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` from that line and
    `dpkg-deb` clamps every archive member to it, which is why two builds of
    these bytes, one on a runner and one here, come out identical.
    `docs/packaging.md` keeps the digest census of the pair - the tag's CI
    build against two builds made from this tag on this bench - and that
    section lands as the commit after the tag, exactly as the 0.5 record
    did, because a digest read before the build that produced it would be a
    number nobody built.

  * The layout editor can add a row, remove one, and change which row is on
    top.  Four controls sit under the fields - `add`, `remove`, `up`,
    `down` - and each entry of the add menu says, before it is pressed,
    which row the new one copies.  A new row is always a copy of a row that
    already draws: this layout's own row of that kind if it has one,
    otherwise a real row from the 28 layouts the editor opens, with this
    repo's five exports ranked first because every row in them is carried to
    a zero-pixel diff against the picture it exports.  No default table was
    written, because an honest one is not available: the vendor's
    application bundles are not in this tree - only their file names are, in
    PROTOCOL.md section 10 - so the files are the only evidence of what a
    row of a kind looks like.

  * What that evidence says, re-counted from the files on every run rather
    than trusted from a page: all 185 rows in the 28 layouts carry exactly
    the five keys `id`, `layerIndex`, `type`, `property`, `widgetContent`,
    `property` is a one-element list in every one of them, and
    `widgetContent` is `""` in all of them.  A new row carries the
    7 / 7 / 11 / 14 / 7 / 9 keys that `text` / `data` / `bar` / `arc` /
    `special` / `line` rows always carry, and every one of its values is a
    value rows of that kind already use.  A `special` row is refused without
    a name from the five drawings `themes.SPECIALS` registers, because a
    `special` nothing draws is no picture rather than a thinner one.  Ids
    follow the spelling of the file being edited - UUIDs in the vendor's,
    `xigma-...` in ours - and stay unique inside a layout, which
    `themes.validate` asks only softly (the vendor's 152 rows in 27 files
    answer to 54 distinct ids, one id shared by 18 of those rows) and the
    model obeys it anyway.

  * Where a new row lands is decided by the painter's own boxes.  A trial
    draw with the row in it reports the box that row actually paints - an
    anchor does not mean the same thing for every kind and every
    `xigma.anchor` - and that box is walked over the canvas in 4 px steps to
    the nearest free position.  Where there is no such position the row
    stays where its source sits and the sentence says so out loud; the row
    is left selected so the reader can drag it.  Quietly moving a row nobody
    asked to move, and parking one out of sight without saying, are both the
    silent repair this model refuses elsewhere.  How an add ends follows
    this machine's faces, so the counts are labelled pairs: the 159 adds
    across the 28 layouts come out 35 / 109 / 15 - clear where their source
    sits, moved to the nearest free spot, still meeting a box - on the
    author's bench, and 35 / 109 / 15 on GitHub's runner.  The reorder pair
    differs by two rows' worth of ink: 120 adjacent pairs whose boxes never
    meet here and 118 there, and of the 12 whose boxes do meet, 6 moved ink
    and 6 did not, on either machine.

  * A reorder moves no ink: the order of `osd.widget` is the draw order, and
    what a swap does is proved with pixels in both directions.  Over every
    adjacent pair of drawn rows in all 28 layouts, a swap changes the frame
    exactly where the two rows both cover some pixel opaquely and in
    different colours.  Pairs whose bounding boxes never meet must swap 0 px,
    and the gate fails if one of them moves a pixel; pairs whose boxes meet
    but whose ink never both lie opaque in the overlap also swap 0 px, which
    is what an `arc`'s box - mostly the hole its ring is drawn around - and
    two vendor `line` rows crossing in sub-pixel coverage look like.
    `layerIndex` is left exactly where it was: over the vendor's files it
    equals the row's position in 23 of their 123 rows and differs in 100,
    nothing in `layout.py` reads it, and inventing a meaning the files do not
    show would be a repair rather than a fix.

  * The last row of a layout cannot be removed, in the library's own words:
    the model empties a copy of the layout, asks `themes.validate` what that
    costs, and repeats its answer - "an `osd.widget` list with nothing in
    it: there is nothing to draw, which is not the same as an empty canvas"
    - rather than paraphrasing it.  `remove` stays disabled at one row, so a
    reader never meets that sentence for the first time at save.  Every one
    of the four acts is exactly one undo step, and the steps are
    last-in-first-out: one press after a remove puts the row back with the
    reorder still standing, the next takes the reorder back, and the frame
    says which of the two was undone.  A refusal - a kind nothing draws, an
    unnamed `special`, a move past either end, the last row - leaves
    `dirty`, the undo count and the file exactly where they were found, and
    the suite checks that rather than hoping.

  * One digit this cycle wrote down wrong, and the gate returned it before
    it shipped.  The sentence was "those 23 files' 123 rows answer to 54
    distinct ids": the 54 belongs to the whole vendor library, across 27
    files and 152 rows, while the 23 layouts this editor can open hold 123
    rows and 25 ids.  Both figures are counted in the run now and held
    against the page, so the pair cannot drift apart again.

  * A duplicated sentence in docs/gui.md - the two lines about
    `xigma.showIf` getting the same menu as `source`, printed twice in one
    bullet - went out with 0.5 and is gone.  It was found while putting the
    four bullets about structural editing into that list, which is what
    reading a page cover to cover does that reading a diff does not.

  * The window's sizing figures move, in height only. An `arc`'s 18 field
    rows stood 796 px tall in one column and 403 in three on the author's
    bench at 0.5, 827 and 434 after the row of row-level controls, and 858
    and 465 after the name box; GitHub's runner read 723 and 378, then 751
    and 406, then 779 and 434. The row that tells a running display to draw
    the open layout is the third such row: 889 and 496 on this bench, the
    runner reading 807 and 462, where that row costs the same 28 px the two
    earlier rows cost there - the runner's figures come out of its own
    failing gate, not a prediction. So each of those three rows costs 31 px
    of height here and 28 px there, and no column's width - and the grid's
    own ask does not move at all (171 / 344 / 512 px here, 180 / 361 / 533
    there), because the grid of rows is the grid that was already there. The
    pair gate is what found this: the first run of this tree failed on the
    bench at the gate the suite calls `the arc box heights`, a later one
    failed on the runner at the same gate, and each failure printed that
    machine's own pair. The three columns' floors, the controls floor by
    columns of rows, the fresh split, the narrowest column and the window's
    own minimum are unchanged on both machines, and the suite still fails
    any pane that has to scroll sideways to be read.

  * lintian has been printing one warning that no CI run has ever seen,
    because the CI job builds binary-only (`-us -uc -b`) while a source
    package build reaches the source checks:
    `missing-license-paragraph-in-dep5-copyright gpl-3.0-or-later+other
    [debian/copyright:37]`, which makes `--fail-on error,warning` exit 2.
    The `docs/previews/*` stanza names that combined licence - our drawing
    over one frame of a vendor clip - and no paragraph of that name existed
    to answer for it.  One does now, and it says what is true: GPL covers
    our half of those files and nothing else, and the combination is
    redistributed on the assessment the OTHER paragraph already states.
    Checked both ways on this bench: `dpkg-buildpackage -S -us -uc -d -i -I`
    at `v0.5` plus lintian on the `.dsc` answers that warning and exit 2;
    the same build with the paragraph added answers 0 errors, 0 warnings.

  * A row's edge resizes it, and the drag writes the size keys the kind
    actually owns. The pane paints eight small squares on the selected row's
    box, and `edit.Editor.size_edges` decides which of them exist by putting
    every edge through the model's own resize door, so a handle the model
    would refuse is never offered. All eight on a `bar`, whose box is `x,y`
    to `x+width,y+height`. All eight on a `line`, the one kind decided
    without drawing: `layout.line_rect` predicts the drawn box of 4 of its 4
    rows exactly, and on a row turned 90 degrees the horizontal extent comes
    from `height` - which is also why `x`/`y` are corrected from that
    prediction when the rect's own pivot, or a `lineWidth` recentring the
    thickness, puts the box somewhere other than where the drag left it. All
    eight on an `arc`, scaling the ring with its box, because `radius` and
    the stroke's `width` move the ink and leave the box where it was (a +8
    px push on `radius` moved the box 0 px and the picture by thousands of
    pixels), and keeping `sizeWidth == 2*radius + 2*width` - the arithmetic
    6 of the 6 arc rows in the library obey, all 6 of them square, so a one-
    axis drag says the box is no longer square rather than squaring it back.
    Six on a `text` or `data` row, which owns no width: not one of the 145
    carries a `width` or `height` key, so that drag searches faces - bracket
    the height, then bisect, drawing every candidate - and quotes the box
    that reached instead of the one that was asked. Four on a `special`,
    because its two axes are two different keys: `width` is the pitch
    between the figures, `fontSize` their face, and a corner has no meaning
    there.

  * Two keys no handle writes, measured rather than listed. `cornerRadius`
    is carried by 25 of the `bar`/`arc` rows and pushing it +8 left every
    box those rows have exactly where it was; `rotate` is carried by 12 `bar`
    rows and changed 0 px on all 12 of them, because nothing in `layout.py`
    reads it on a `bar`. Where a cap owns the extent, the drag is refused in
    that cap's name - `xigma.autoFitMax` fits a row to a width,
    `xigma.fitArc` fits a `special`'s figures into a ring - and the sentence
    says which of the two it is, and that the row to change is that one. A
    row that draws no box at this fixture has no edge to grab, and the
    reason comes from the painter rather than from a paraphrase of it.

  * The pane promises the ask; the model returns the result. While the mouse
    is down the pane paints, in a solid line beside the dashed box that is,
    the box `edit.Editor.resize_box` says the release would ask for - the
    same door `resize_row` answers through, so no second copy of that
    arithmetic lives in the window. A repaint in the middle of a drag (the
    note line growing, the splitter refitting the pane) puts that ask back
    rather than dropping it. A square belongs to the selected row even under
    another row's ink, because "the later row wins an overlap" is a rule
    about ink, and the note says whose ink is lying under the square. One
    press, drag and release is exactly one undo step, moves pixels, and
    writes no file: every accepted drag the suite drove put the layout back
    byte for byte under one press of undo, and every refusal left `dirty`,
    the undo count and the picture where they were found.

  * A copy can be renamed, and one layout can be saved as a second copy
    under a new name. Two buttons over a name box, `themes.rename_copy` and
    `edit.Editor.save_as` behind them, and one rule shared by every door
    that invents a name (`themes.new_name`): the name has to clear three
    things at once, because `themes.resolve()` matches three - the 27 layout
    names in the library, those names folded, and the 27 ids inside the
    files. That is measured rather than cautious: a copy put at a vendor's
    own name takes that layout out of reach by name *and* by id
    (`resolve(<that id>)` answers None afterwards), and a copy named
    `theme_07` beside the vendor's `Theme_07` leaves `--list-themes` showing
    two layouts while every spelling resolves to one file. A name is one
    path element, at most 64 characters, never one of the five drawn from
    `render.py`, and never ending in `.json`, `.bak` or `.tmp` - the
    suffixes this program writes beside a layout, so a layout called
    `x.json` would live at `x.json.json` while the `x.json` next to it is
    the layout named `x`; none of the 27 names here does, so that rule
    refuses nothing that exists. Only a copy of yours can be renamed, and
    the name box has no length cap on purpose: a box that truncated at 64
    would act on a name nobody typed, so the door refuses with its own
    sentence instead.

  * A rename changes one path and one key, and says both. The rows, the
    canvas and the background are untouched - the suite draws the layout
    before and after and requires the pixels to match - and it is a file
    act, not an edit: no undo step, and `dirty` exactly as it was. The `id`
    inside the file does change, to a fresh 13-digit number in the shape the
    vendor's own files use (their 27 fall inside 1 187 564 ms of one
    another, which is the stamp of an application saving a layout), because
    `--theme <id>` answers a layout by its number as well as its name and
    two files sharing one is a name space with a hole in it.
    `<name>.json.bak` travels with the copy so undoing the last save is
    still that save; a half-written `.tmp` is reported and left where it is.
    `save as` leaves the first file byte for byte and its id, and it is also
    how a layout drawn from `render.py` gets a file of its own: `gauge.json`
    is still refused as a second `theme_gauge`, while a copy named something
    else is yours and `--theme gauge` keeps drawing the code's picture.

  * Which file answers a name is asked, never remembered. The editor's state
    line, the layout menu's entries and numbers, and the revert sentence all
    read `themes.resolve()` on the current name - the same door `--list-
    themes`, the loop's log line and the control socket's `theme` command go
    through - because the three moments that move a file are exactly the
    moments a remembered word goes stale; after reverting a renamed copy the
    honest line is `no file answers this name`. The menu is rebuilt with its
    signals blocked, because selecting the entry for the layout already in
    memory would re-read the file and throw away what was typed since the
    last save. A loop re-resolves its name every frame, so a rename reaches
    it on the next frame, and if the name was yours alone then nothing
    answers it: `render.draw` falls back to `gauge`, measured pixel for
    pixel, which is why the note tells the reader to re-point the loop over
    the socket - while a fresh `--theme` of that dead name exits 2 with
    `themes.why_not`'s sentence. Two related corrections: the loop's own
    report called a layout in `~/.config/xigmalcd/themes` "the installed
    package", which is now `themes.origin()`'s wording, and
    `edit.Editor.open_theme` handed the editor the object `themes.load()`
    caches, so an unsaved keystroke reached what `scan()`, `pair()` and
    `describe()` read.

  * A drag can be pulled onto a number that same layout already uses. Press
    a row's ink, move it, and if the landing comes within 2 px of a line
    this file offers, the row goes onto it and the note line says whose
    number it is: another row's declared `x`, another row's `y`, and on x
    the middle of the canvas and the mirror of another row's centre. The
    reach is counted rather than chosen: of the 185 rows that carry an `x`
    or a `y` in the 28 layouts this pane opens, 89 hold an `x` another row
    of the same file holds exactly and 44 do the same with `y` - 317 pairs
    of rows holding one number between them - while the rest are apart on
    purpose: the nearest other row's `x` is 1 px away for 0 of those rows, 2
    px away for 12, and 3 px away for 4, and nothing sits 6 or 7 px off. So
    2 px is the break between "meant to line up" and "spaced". Three is not:
    a 3 px drag is pulled 11% of the time at 1 px, 12% at 2 px and 57% at 3
    px, because rows sharing a column sit within 3 px of each other's
    landing - a snap arguing with the person dragging.

  * What a line is allowed to mean: the same anchor class. A row's class is
    the first letter of `xigma.anchor` (`l`, `m` or `r`; the vendor's
    default is `la`), and that letter is what says where `x` sits on the
    glass - measured against the painter on every run: a left-anchored row's
    `x` is exactly its ink's left edge, a right-anchored row's is exactly
    its right edge, a centre-anchored row's lies between the two. Across
    classes the two rows' ink would sit most of a canvas apart, so the model
    refuses the line rather than writing a number that only looks aligned. A
    row with a declared width offers its far edge too (`x + width` for a
    `bar`, `x + sizeWidth` for an `arc`, the rect `layout.line_rect` draws
    for a `line`), and a `special` pair row offers its two figures at `x -
    width/2` and `x + width/2`. The files chose the rest: 13 rows declare
    `x` exactly 240 and 220 pairs declare two `x` values summing to the 480
    the same file declares, while on y 3 rows sit at the middle and 8 pairs
    mirror - so the centre and the mirror are offered on x only. And no row
    anywhere declares `x` or `y` at 0 or at 479, which is why the canvas
    edges stay the bounds `move_to` refuses to cross rather than becoming
    lines to line up on. For a row that is not centre-anchored, the number
    that centres it comes from the box the painter draws, and the sentence
    says `at this fixture`, because a `data` row's digits change and its ink
    moves with them while its `x` stays where the file wrote it.

  * A row leaves a line the way it arrived: by dragging off it. A line the
    row was already sitting on is not offered back, so a 2 px drag away from
    a shared column sticks - which is what the corpus asks for, and why
    there is no Shift to hold and no checkbox to remember. While a pull is
    on, the pane draws that line across the glass and the model's own
    sentence goes under the rows during the drag and again at the release;
    the line is chrome and nothing else, so the picture the pane saves is
    byte for byte what it is with the guide off, and the drag is still
    exactly one undo step because the snap happens inside `move_to` rather
    than around it. Three things are not snapped, and the pages say so: the
    arrow keys (a 1 px nudge pulled back is a keypress that did nothing the
    cursor asked for), the typed field rows (a number typed is a number
    wanted), and a resize drag (feature D's edges write a size, and a
    neighbour's edge is not this row's size). And there is no grid to snap
    to, nor was one invented: the gcd of every declared `x` and of every
    declared `y` is 1, and only 27 of the 185 declared `x` values are
    multiples of 16.

  * The editor's pane can now look at another body's readings without
    editing in them: a folded box called **Pane readings**, under the
    advanced switch, offers `the fixture`, `this machine's readings`, and `a
    metrics file`. A fresh window opens at the pinned fixture and the peek
    is never remembered - no key for it goes near the session file - so a
    window you did not choose cannot come up showing readings it grabbed by
    itself. The file entry takes the same `STATE all 1` JSON shape as
    `--metrics-file` and reads it through the same door,
    `main.read_metrics_file`, because one file shape has to mean one thing
    in one program: its sections replace whole sections of what this machine
    answers, a `_`-prefixed key is a comment in the file rather than a
    section, and a file that is missing or broken is refused in words. A
    peek sends nothing: it paints, it does not upload.
    The fold census moves with the new box: 12 secondary controls in 5 rows and
    3 boxes - named by the switch's note, its tooltip, docs/gui.md's table, and
    the gate that lists every folded widget one by one.

  * The peek is held and its instant stays pinned, which is what makes it
    one picture instead of a slideshow. The readings are read once, when the
    entry is chosen, and again only when the reader chooses the entry they are
    already on - that press *is* the re-read; a pane that followed the sensors
    at 2 fps would move as far as this box's readings happen to move - nothing
    at all on a box whose sensors sit still - so the gate times one read, one
    draw and one second of following, and prints the milliseconds and the
    moved pixels it measured instead of this file quoting a number that
    belongs to one machine. The instant is pinned because that half is a fact
    about the files: the painter takes `Date`, `Time` and `Week` from the
    instant rather than from any reading, the library's rows ask for 2 of
    those 3 names and 17 rows read them, so a live clock rewrites those rows
    every second. What the pane is looking at is said in three places: the
    model's own sentence under the rows, the badge under the picture (`pane:
    PEEK - …`), and the caption.

  * A peek cannot be edited in, and it says so rather than failing quietly.
    The dashed box, the eight resize squares and any snap guide are hidden
    while a peek is up, because they are drawn from the pinned row's edges
    while the ink on screen sits elsewhere - the suite counts, on the
    machine it runs on, how many row boxes move at another body's readings
    and how many of the points it probes on the glass answer a different row
    in one frame than the other, and refuses to print either count unless
    the difference is actually there. A press on the peek, and an arrow key,
    are refused with `edit.Editor.peek_refusal`'s own words - the window
    does not paraphrase the model - naming the knob that puts the editable
    frame back; the undo count, the `dirty` flag, the selected row's `x` and
    the file's bytes do not move. The note lines under the fields do follow
    the peek, because the measured difference is in values only (`57°C`
    becomes `104°C` at a file body pinning `cpu.temperature` to 104.0) with
    the reason strings left alone. Feature D's squares are asked of a box, and
    `size_edges` never consults a reading - one box, one answer, which is what
    the gate asserts per row - but the box is what the painter drew, so a
    second body can change which squares exist to grab at all; the rows whose
    offer moved are printed with the boxes that moved them, rather than
    counted on a page.

  * One loader rather than two. `--metrics-file`'s rule - what a section is,
    what a comment key is, what a broken file means - lived in
    `main._pinned`; the peek needed the same rule, so it is now
    `main.read_metrics_file(path) -> (sections, reason)` and `_pinned` is
    its caching wrapper. The loop keeps its last good read and logs once per
    change of a broken file; the window puts the sentence on the note line,
    keeps the pinned frame painted, and leaves the mode the reader chose on
    the combo instead of snapping the control back under their fingers. A
    file that names no path says so, and the chosen mode waits for the path.

  * Gates for all of it, in the same twenty-two offline sections, no port
    opened by any of them.  The new checks take their shape from the files
    on this run - the envelope, the mandatory key sets, the id reuse, the
    `layerIndex` census - and hold the sentences in docs/gui.md,
    docs/limits.md and `edit.py`'s own comments to that count.  A figure
    that follows a face is printed with the machine it was read on, and the
    page labels whose machine each half of the pair belongs to; a run that
    matches neither half fails rather than loosening the claim.  The resize
    gates add the per-kind sweep - every drawn row, every edge its own rules
    call live, the arc identity re-checked after each accepted drag, and the
    box followed 1:1 wherever the geometry is declared - and the three `line`
    cases no shipped file carries, built in memory: a `rotate: 90` row with a
    `lineWidth`, a plain `lineWidth`, and a row whose extent belongs to a cap.
    The window half drives its own marks: the squares it paints are the ones
    the model named, each square grabs its own edge, the ask on the glass is
    that door's answer, the cursor says which way the row grows, and both the
    refusal and the overlap are said in the pane's note line.  The naming gates
    hold the rule to the library rather than to a list - 27 names, all distinct
    folded, and 27 ids, all distinct - measure a rename for changing exactly one
    key and moving no pixel, check that a fresh id clears every id on disk, and
    drive both buttons through the window: the menu's entries and its tooltip
    numbers recounting from `themes.scan()`, the name box keeping what was
    typed, nine refusals leaving every byte of the one copy on disk where it
    was, and the passage ending with the user root as empty as it found it.
    The snapping gates count the corpus from its declared numbers - 185 rows, 89
    and 44 of them sharing one, 317 pairs, the 1 px bucket holding nothing
    at all, the blast radius at 1 / 2 / 3 px - and hold `edit.py`'s
    comments, this stanza and docs/gui.md to the same digits, which is what
    makes them figures about the files rather than one machine's readings.
    Then they drive the drag itself: the anchor class measured against the
    painter, a pull onto a neighbour's exact number, one pixel outside the
    reach moving nothing, the line you left not pulling you back, cross-
    class lines not offered at all, the mirror proved by drawing the row
    there and seeing the two box centres sum to the width the file declares,
    a guide line that leaves the picture the pane saves unchanged byte for
    byte, an arrow key answering 1 px and no more, and two presses of undo
    going back over the nudge and then over the drag.
    The peek's gates drive the window's own knob: a named file body drawn
